  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "If \\(x\\ge0,\\) then \\(\\sqrt{x\\sqrt{x\\sqrt{x}}}=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x\\sqrt{x}\\)",
    "B": "\\(x\\sqrt[4]{x}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt[8]{x}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt[8]{x^3}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt[8]{x^7}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Work from the innermost square root outward. For \\(x>0\\), taking a square root halves an exponent, while multiplying powers of \\(x\\) adds their exponents. Thus the middle radical is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{x\\sqrt{x}}=(x\\cdot x^{1/2})^{1/2}=x^{3/4}.\n\\]\nThe full expression is therefore\n\\[\n\\sqrt{x\\sqrt{x\\sqrt{x}}}\n=(x\\cdot x^{3/4})^{1/2}\n=x^{7/8}\n=\\sqrt[8]{x^7}.\n\\]\nWhen \\(x=0\\), the original expression and \\(\\sqrt[8]{x^7}\\) both equal \\(0\\). Hence the answer for every \\(x\\geq0\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt[8]{x^7}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
